## Current boundaries

Repository instructions, role frontmatter, Claude permission patterns, CI permissions, branch policy, environment separation, and human approval rules provide current guardrails. A deterministic role/path/action gateway is now the engineering-agent pre-tool boundary; prompt settings remain defense in depth.

The machine-readable manifest makes role tools, forbidden action classes, data limits, approvals, and handoffs testable. The Claude PreToolUse hook invokes the same gateway used by standalone policy tests.

## Deterministic policy gateway

Before a tool executes, the gateway evaluates run/role identity, tool, command class, requested path, allowed scope, and dependency authority, then returns `allow`, `ask`, or `deny`. It fails closed for secret access, history rewrite, recursive deletion, scope escape, direct deploy, reviewer/QA mutation, and tested shell-indirection forms. Commit, push, PR mutation, and workflow dispatch require `ask` unless the role is forbidden entirely.

The hook cannot replace server-side branch/environment controls. Production remains blocked until its external approval boundary is non-bypassable.

## Stable boundaries

- data classification is part of every work request;
- a provider is rejected before scoring if policy or capability is insufficient;
- planning and review are read-only;
- implementation writes only inside its assigned scope; automatic isolated-worktree enforcement remains target state;
- secrets and production credentials are never included in Markdoc context;
- workers cannot push, merge, deploy, or approve their own work;
- legal, privacy, spending, architecture, and production risk stop at a human gate.

{% human_gate kind="security and production" %}
High-risk ambiguity must be confirmed before routing. A confident model response does not replace authorization.
{% /human_gate %}

